h2. Overview

Following the work in AE-84, we need to provide two AMD modules to give developers a clean, consistent API for working with ESM modules and React components from AMD code.

----

h2. 1. {{core/import}}

A thin AMD module that exposes a dynamic ESM import helper, working around the static analysis restrictions that prevent {{import()}} from being used directly inside AMD modules.

{code:js}export default new Function('specifier', 'return import(specifier)');{code}

This allows AMD callers to do:

{code:js}import nativeImport from 'core/import';
nativeImport('@moodle/lms/mod_book/viewer').then(...);{code}

----

h2. 2. {{core/component}}

An AMD module (or addition to {{core/templates}}) that provides a consistent API for mounting ESM/React components into the DOM by creating the expected container markup.

h3. {{appendToDom(moduleName, properties, appendTo)}}

Creates a {{<div>}} with the appropriate {{data-react-component}} and {{data-react-props}} attributes and appends it to the given element.

{code:js}export const appendToDom = (moduleName, properties, appendTo) => {
    const container = document.createElement('div');
    container.dataset.reactComponent = moduleName;
    container.dataset.reactProps = JSON.stringify(properties);
    appendTo.append(container);
};{code}

h3. {{prependToDom(moduleName, properties, prependTo)}}

Same as {{appendToDom}} but prepends the container instead.

----

h2. Acceptance criteria

* {{core/import}} exists as an AMD module and successfully returns a Promise resolving to an ESM module.
* {{core/component}} (or equivalent location in {{core/templates}}) exposes both {{appendToDom}} and {{prependToDom}}.
* Both functions create the correct container markup recognised by the MutationObserver auto-initialiser.
* Unit/Behat coverage as appropriate.

Modify the import map and page requirements manager to add import map entries for each theme, mapping a pattern like {{@moodle/lms/[themename]}} to a versioned URL such as:

{noformat}https://example.com/sm/core/esm/1776735946/[themename]/@moodle/lms/{noformat}

The current page should also resolve to the active theme's URL, so that:

{noformat}@moodle/lms/core/example => https://example.com/sm/core/esm/1776735946/boost/@moodle/lms/core/example{noformat}

Theme resolution must be deferred to request time — not performed up-front on every page load, as scanning all possible theme override files is an expensive operation.

This also needs to consider whether swizzling of other component libraries (e.g. MDS) should be supported in the same way.

In the page requirements manager, {{react_mustache_autoinit}} currently specifies a file path for the autoinit script. It should instead use the bare module specifier from the import map.

The correct implementation is:

{code:php}public function react_mustache_autoinit(): string {
    return html_writer::tag(
        tagname: 'script',
        contents: 'import "@moodle/lms/core/react_autoinit";',
        attributes: [
            'type' => 'module',
        ],
    );
}{code}

Using a bare specifier (pulled from the import map) is consistent with how other modules are loaded and ensures the correct versioned URL is resolved by the browser at runtime rather than hardcoded in PHP.

User validation takes place in {{public/webservice/lib.php}} in the {{webservice_server::authenticate_user()}} method.

I propose moving the various parts of this out into a new class: {{\core_auth\validate_user}}, breaking up the differenet constituent parts into different methods, for example:

* {{validate_user_maintenance_mode_access($user)}}
* {{validate_user_not_deleted($user)}}
* {{validate_user_is_confirmed($user)}}
* {{validate_user_is_not_suspended($user)}}

We can then have some other methods like:

* {{validate_user_before_external_login($user)}}

Each of these validations should return void or throw a specific exception:

* {{\core_auth\exception\maintenance_mode_enabled_exception($user)}}
* {{\core_auth\exception\user_deleted_exception($user)}}
* {{\core_auth\exception\user_not_confirmed_exception($user)}}
* {{\core_auth\exception\user_suspended_exception($user)}}

I’ve previously created a PoC of this change here: [https://github.com/andrewnicols/moodle/commit/2ddb56f3f2a52bbd066ed52bd7016ba74d294bc8|https://github.com/andrewnicols/moodle/commit/2ddb56f3f2a52bbd066ed52bd7016ba74d294bc8|smart-link], but this may not be complete or correct - especially around the exception handling.

This change relies on [https://moodle.atlassian.net/browse/MDL-88579|https://moodle.atlassian.net/browse/MDL-88579|smart-link] to pass the previous exception in the chain.

I’ve just encountered and issue whereby a change in a parent class causes build changes in the child classes, for example:

Parent class:

{{Base.ts}}

{noformat}export default class Base {
    get(key: string): any {
        return (this as any)[key];
    }
}{noformat}



{{Child.ts}}

{noformat}import Base from './Base';

export default class Child extends Base {
    get(key: string): any {
        return (this as any)[key];
    }
}{noformat}



After building these changes, and staging then, I added a {{set}} method to the {{Base.ts}} too:

{noformat}    set(key: string, value: any): void {
        (this as any)[key] = value;
    }{noformat}



Upon building I now get changes to the Child.min.ts.

This is because esbuild is automatically bundling relative imports. We need to mark all files as {{external}}.
